can tell is that we have  sort of two  ways of thinking  the fast way and the slow way the fast  way is our intuitive way of thinking  it's uh  very much influenced by  um  our experience  i guess  patterns that we've built up in our  uh prejudices perhaps  we jump to conclusions and he  demonstrates just how irrational so much  of our thinking is  um  you know you'd have to read the book to  see all the different examples we're  very much influenced by  um  you know if we describe someone as being  energetic enthusiastic and stubborn  we have a better impression of that  person than if you know if we say  energetic enthusiastic stubborn  and envious  we may still think that person's a good  person because we started out with  positive terms if we turn it around and  we begin with envious and stubborn then  the enthusiastic and energetic uh  doesn't seem to overcome those first  attributes that we gave the person  that's just one example that comes from  from memory there are a number of others  and that our tendency is to want to use  the the easy way of thinking what he  calls cognitive ease  we want if we're asked a complicated  question we'll substitute that for a  simple question that we can easily  answer because that is our quick  thinking our slow thinking is more  complicated it's furrowed brow it's it's  trying to make complicated calculations  and and if if we are driving a car if  we're multitasking it's very easy to  combine the sort of easy thinking tasks  uh with some other activity however if  we have to calculate you know 37 by 43  we can't make that calculation in our  head while driving a car for example  anything that we are already prepared  conditioned to respond to  those easy cognitive things we can do  those while we're driving a car  and he talks also about how um you know  typically  uh people are far too overconfident  about how  uh  you know their ability to achieve  projects uh you know  deliver any major project on on budget  and on time and all of these things  because we're always optimistic we're  always overconfident we don't really  analyze all of the facts at hand  we tend to be carried away with  the sort of  prejudices that we have going in and our  our desire to succeed and our  overconfidence and so forth so it's  quite an interesting book and i mean i  don't do it justice here and i have to  read it again to fully understand it  but  he also talks about happiness and how  for example we have a tendency to  place greater emphasis on the peaks of  happiness or suffering rather than the  duration  so we are  we tend to forget you know that we were  in pain for you know 10 minutes but the  peaks were low whereas if we had very  high level of pain for a very short  period of time that's worth  worse and the same is true in reverse we  tend to remember the sort of peaks of  enjoyment rather than the longer period  of happiness and so he questions to some  extent  uh all these different evaluations of  happiness and how value how how you know  valid they are  uh and so forth but what i take out of  all of this is the following  first of all insofar as language  learning is concerned  the more i can do with my quick learning  pre you know pre-prepared you know um  patterns that are already created in my  mind easy cognitive the more things that  i do that way the better off i am the  more i enjoy my language learning  we had someone at our forum at link come  on and say you know the  because that link we cache every time  one of our users goes to a dictionary  whichever dictionary and and selects a  meaning for a word from you know spanish  into english that's cached in the system  not all of those hints are necessarily  appropriate and they don't provide you  information on the tense or the  conjugations and so forth so someone's  saying this is not valid we need to have  all the conjugations there we need to  have this that and the other and i say  well not for me because  really i just want to focus on the  content that i'm reading and listening  to so i get a suggestion of roughly what  the word means and i try to piece it  together from the context and that's  really all i want i don't want to bother  myself with all the  you know conjugations and stuff because  half i know from experience that i tend  to forget that stuff pretty pretty  quickly so i'd rather just focus on the  easy cognitive things  okay yeah it roughly means that so  therefore in this context it seems to  mean this so i kind of am getting some  sense out of the context without having  to think about it too much if i listen  to it i can listen to it while i'm doing  the dishes i don't have to  focus on trying to remember anything  it's just easy cognitive stuff and when  i'm speaking the language i just go to  that  easy cognitive whatever reflex i have  built up in there be it right be it  wrong that's what i go with i don't  worry too much about trying to remember  the rules is this correct or not correct  and i find that that this sort of if we  can develop  our little our knowledge of the language  uh  again through ma as crash and says  through massive listening and reading we  develop these  these patterns we develop these sort of  intuitive reactions  some of which are right some of which  are wrong and and uh  kahneman and his book makes the point  that a lot of our intuitive  uh views on things are are  false or wrong or distorted it doesn't  matter we need these we operate on the  basis of these intuitive reactions these  this  quick thinking that we have and i think  the same is true of language we need to  develop that side of it  personally the more i rely on  the uh slow think work it through  analyze and stuff the less enjoyment i  have in my language learning and i think  the less well i speak  uh  so and and so that's one thing that  comes out of it the other thing that he  mentions is that uh  you know when we look at what makes us  happy  one of the things is the ability to  focus  on the moment that we are in  we can't be happy if we're studying  french and we think we should be  studying korean  we have to totally savor the moment  savor the language that we are studying  or if we would rather be  you know out dancing then we should be  out dancing uh so that the idea that you  you savor the moment that you're in  is is important to uh  to achieving happiness and i think the  same is true in language learning that  you have to  you know  basically commit yourself to the task at  hand and enjoy the task at hand derive  pleasure from the task at hand rather  than  thinking that you're doing something  that's gonna you know  bring you some benefits later on so it's  unpleasant and it's hard work and you're  struggling and stuff but you're hoping  that somehow that this is going to  uh  get you to where you want to be  i found that  if the approach to language learning is  this is fun i'm enjoying listening to  whatever it is i'm enjoying reading it  i'm joined being in the language in the  moment that that's really all you need  to do without thinking of where it's  going to lead to  and and then when you do occasionally  have that sense that you've made a major  step forward which we  every so often feel even though for for  long periods of time we think we're not  improving then there are moments when  you think you do then you should really  savor those  uh moments without worrying about the  mistakes that you made  without worrying about  um  whether you can remember you know  endings of verbs and stuff none of that  really matters  so  um i guess  really i i i it may not be very clear  and i think i have to go back in to read  the book again and i would be very happy  to discuss this with some of you who may  choose to read the book again i'll show  you the book here  uh  thinking fast and slow daniel kahneman  but i think there's a lot in there that  i have to go back and read again and  again  uh which demonstrates just how faulty a  lot of our thinking is but but that's  the way we are the other big thing in  canaan is his emphasis on  on luck any in any success  luck has a major role to play and  therefore if we have a a string of  success there's every likelihood that  this will be followed by a string of  failures or vice versa because  because luck is such a big factor there  is this effect of what he calls the  regression to the mean  so that  inevitably statistically if you've had a  lot of success through luck you're going  to have some periods of less success and  so we we need to accept that as well  but there's a lot of lessons to life in  that book it's time seems a bit cut and  dried statistical analysis  prospect theory uh you know uh decision  making theory and stuff like that  but i think there's a lot there that's  that's valid in general and in  particular valid for  for language learning so  it's kind of a bit of a fuzzy  rant today but i think there is  something there  uh  at least in in my own experience  and those people who like to get into  the nitty-gritty of grammar and enjoy  doing that and that's their thing  they're in the flow they're enjoying it  by all means go for it  but i think a lot of people don't  and even if you don't there are so many  ways that you can enjoy language  learning  where you're not worried about where you  stand vis-a-vis other people you're not  worried about where you stand vis-a-vis  some future goal you have you're just  enjoying being in the moment you're  enjoying  this sort of  cognitive ease of just listening and  reading  in the knowledge that in fact it's  building up a lot of experience  in your brain a lot of patterns that  eventually will become intuition some  may be wrong some may be right but you  can flow with that intuition and you're  building up an ability to react easily  quickly  and even in a way that you can multitask  in the language  and that that's happiness in language  learning and it's not more complicated  than that and you don't have to worry  about whether you're a b1 b2 c1 toeic  toefl and all the rest of it that will  all come of its own  if you can  sort of um  just invest of yourself  in into the language in this in this  sort of natural learning process so i'm  not sure any of this makes sense but  there you go i look forward to your  comments  bye for now

